\title{Ruler and Compass Constructions}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{JB00}
\import{macros}

\p{We use our results on field extensions to show that certain classical problems concerning \em{ruler and compass constructions} cannot be solved.}

\p{Let #{S \subset \mathbb{R}^2} be a finite set of points. We may}

\ul{
\li{\strong{(i)} draw a straight line through any 2 distinct points in #{S};}
\li{\strong{(ii)} draw a circle with centre any point in #{S} and radius the distance between 2 points in #{S};}
\li{\strong{(iii)} enlarge #{S} by adjoining any point of intersection of 2 distinct lines/circles (as drawn in (i) and (ii)).}
}

\transclude{GA0X}

\transclude{GA0Y}

\p{We will relate this definition to the following.}

\transclude{GA0Z}

\transclude{GA10}

\transclude{GA11}

\transclude{GA12}

\transclude{GA13}

\p{\strong{Some classical problems}}

\ul{
\li{\strong{1.} \em{Squaring the circle.} One is to construct a square whose area is the same as that of a circle with unit radius. This amounts to constructing the real number #{\sqrt{\pi}}. This is impossible by \ref{GA13} and the fact that #{\pi} is transcendental (Lindemann).}
\li{\strong{2.} \em{Duplicating the cube.} One is to construct a cube whose volume is twice that of a given cube. This amounts to constructing #{\sqrt[3]{2}}. But #{\sqrt[3]{2}} has minimal polynomial #{X^3 - 2}, so #{[\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt[3]{2}):\mathbb{Q}] = 3}, which is not a power of #{2}.}
\li{\p{\strong{3.} \em{Trisecting the angle.} One is to divide a given angle into #{3} equal angles. Suppose the given angle is #{2\pi /3}. Since this is itself constructible (we can construct an equilateral triangle), if this problem can be solved then the angle #{2\pi /9} is constructible; in other words the real numbers #{\cos\frac{2\pi}{9}}, #{\sin\frac{2\pi}{9}} will be constructible.}
\p{From the formula #{\cos 3\theta = 4\cos^3\theta - 3\cos\theta}, #{2\cos\frac{2\pi}{9}} is a root of #{f(X) = X^3 - 3X + 1}.}
\p{#{f(\pm 1) \neq 0 \implies f} is irreducible in #{\mathbb{Z}[X]} (or apply Eisenstein’s criterion to #{f(X-1) = X^3 - 3X^2 + 3}). Therefore #{f} is irreducible in #{\mathbb{Q}[X]} by Gauss’ Lemma. Therefore #{[\mathbb{Q}(\cos\frac{2\pi}{9}):\mathbb{Q}] = 3}, which is not a power of #{2}. So this construction is impossible.}}
}

\transclude{GA14}
